# credential reader component options (ESP-IDF). Applies to every app that pulls the
# ultrawidelock_reader component (the standalone reader and the Matter lock).

menu "credential reader"

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_LAT_TRACE
	bool "Walk-up latency trace (ultrawidelock-lat budget line)"
	default y
	help
	  One-shot timestamps at every walk-up phase boundary (BLE connect ->
	  discovery -> L2CAP -> AUTH0/EXCHANGE -> M1-M4 -> first range ->
	  threshold -> bolt), printed as a single consolidated "ultrawidelock-lat"
	  budget line after the bolt is driven. Diagnostics only: one uptime
	  read + store per boundary, nothing on the UWB re-arm hot path.
	  Disable to compile the trace out entirely.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_CRED_LAB
	bool "Aliro Lab transaction trace"
	default y
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_LAT_TRACE
	help
	  Compile in the structured "[ALAB] t=<us> ev=..." trace at transaction
	  phase boundaries, so a captured serial log can be turned into a scored
	  walk-up report (phase waterfall, approach curve, checks).
	  Like the sibling `uwbdiag` trace, it ships in every credential build but
	  stays OFF at boot — the `lab on`/`lab off` console command toggles it
	  at runtime, off the UWB hot path. Its per-phase lines come from the
	  ultrawidelock-lat stamps, hence the ULTRAWIDELOCK_LAT_TRACE dependency. Set n to strip
	  it entirely from a hardened production image.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE
	bool "RSSI-gated UWB ranging (power gate)"
	default y
	help
	  Gate the UWB radio on BLE connection RSSI: the reader completes the
	  credential auth as soon as the phone connects, but holds the one message
	  the phone waits for before initiating ranging (Reader-Status-AP-
	  Completed, §11.7.3.4.1) until the smoothed RSSI says the phone is in
	  the last few metres. Until then the DW3000 stays powered off instead
	  of listening continuously from BLE range — the difference between a
	  battery-powered lock and a tethered one. A sustained fade below the
	  close threshold tears ranging down again and drops the link; the
	  phone re-runs the fast auth on its next approach. With this off the
	  radio arms right after auth, exactly as before.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE_OPEN_DBM
	int "Gate open threshold (dBm)"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE
	default -55
	help
	  Smoothed connection RSSI at or above this opens the gate (allows UWB).

	  Set from a measured 274-sample curve, not from separation alone. Ranging
	  needs about 1.5 s from AP-Completed to its first trusted range, so the
	  gate has to open roughly 2 m out or the
	  radio is still warming up when the phone reaches the door. On the bench
	  rig -55 lands in the 2.0-2.5 m band; -50 separates near from far better
	  on paper but opens under 1 m, which arrives too late to be useful.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE_CLOSE_DBM
	int "Gate close threshold (dBm)"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE
	default -65
	help
	  Smoothed RSSI at or below this — sustained for the close hold time —
	  closes the gate: ranging stops, the DW3000 powers down and the link
	  is dropped. Keep well below the open threshold (hysteresis) so body
	  fades cannot flap the radio.

	  10 dB under the open threshold, which on the measured curve puts the
	  close edge past 3 m: you have to walk a metre back out before the radio
	  powers down. The old -75 was below the level seen at any distance the
	  rig could still hold a link at, so the gate effectively never closed.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE_CLOSE_HOLD_MS
	int "Gate close hold (ms)"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE
	default 3000
	help
	  How long the smoothed RSSI must stay below the close threshold before
	  the gate closes. Rides out pocket/body fades mid-approach.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE_MAX_HOLD_MS
	int "Longest AP-Completed hold (ms; 0 = unbounded)"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE
	default 0
	help
	  Cap on how long Reader-Status-AP-Completed may be deferred while the
	  gate stays closed. 0 holds indefinitely, which is the default, and the
	  bench is why.

	  The phone will not wait: measured patience is about 1.9 s, after which
	  it terminates the link (HCI reason 0x13) and reconnects. A capped hold
	  therefore trades that reconnect for arming UWB. Both arms were run.
	  Capping at 1200 ms released the gate at -75 and -72 dBm with the phone
	  still roughly 4 m out, and the DW3000 then listened to nothing for
	  2.8 s in one session and 6.6 s in the next. That is the exact waste the
	  gate exists to prevent, and it buys only the avoidance of a reconnect
	  that costs one fast re-auth, which is BLE-only and about 200 ms.

	  So: hold, let the phone give up, and let it come back when it is closer.
	  The unlock is not at risk, because an unprimed gate still fails open and
	  the phone demonstrably retries.

	  Set a nonzero value to take the other trade. If you do, note the cap is
	  only evaluated when an RSSI sample arrives, so the hold ends up to
	  ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE_POLL_MS late: a 1500 ms cap released at 1695 ms on the
	  bench, leaving 121 ms against the phone's 1873. Budget cap + poll.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE_SLOPE_DB
	int "Fast-open rise (dB per 1.5 s window; 0 disables)"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE
	default 8
	help
	  A rise of this many dB across the tracking window opens the gate
	  early (once above the close threshold), so a fast walk-up is not
	  penalized by the smoothing lag. 0 turns the rise-rate fast open off
	  (threshold-only gating) — useful as the comparison arm in the power/
	  latency study.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE_POLL_MS
	int "Connection RSSI poll period (ms)"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE
	default 250
	help
	  How often the transport reads the controller RSSI while the credential
	  L2CAP channel is up (one HCI Read RSSI per period; negligible next
	  to the UWB RX it replaces). The first read fires at channel-open so
	  a connect-at-the-door walk-up is never delayed by the poll interval.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_CRED_HOUSEKEEPING_STACK_SIZE
	int "Credential housekeeping thread stack size"
	range 2048 8192
	default 3072
	help
	  Static stack, in bytes, for the deferred P-256 spare generation and
	  provisioning persistence (cred_housekeeping). Mirrors the symbol in
	  modules/ultrawidelock_cred/Kconfig, same default, so the constants gate
	  sees one number; without the symbol this port had no way to move off the
	  source fallback of 3072, which mbedTLS's software P-256 keygen overran
	  (stack canary in cred_housekeeping on an ESP32-S3). The Matter lock pins
	  6144 in its sdkconfig.defaults, matching the step-up worker that runs the
	  same curve on this port.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_APPROACH_PREDICT
	bool "Predictive (ETA) unlock — negative latency"
	depends on !ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE
	default y
	help
	  Run the constant-velocity Kalman path in ultrawidelock_approach: estimate the
	  credential's closing speed and start retracting the bolt early, so it
	  finishes at arrival instead of beginning there.

	  It needs a run-up. The ETA only arms while the estimate is still
	  OUTSIDE the unlock radius, with a converged filter and a closing speed
	  above the minimum, so the controller has to see the phone approaching
	  from beyond a metre.

	  That is exactly what ULTRAWIDELOCK_RSSI_GATE takes away, which is why this
	  depends on the gate being off. The gate withholds the message that
	  starts ranging until the phone is already close, and a further ~1.29 s
	  elapses before the first trusted range (1286 and 1289 ms, measured).
	  On the bench the first trusted ranges with the gate on were 48 cm and
	  6 cm — already inside the radius, so the predictor never armed once in
	  69 trusted ranges. Stacking them does not blend the two behaviours; it
	  silently disables this one.

	  Pick per lock: the gate for battery power, this for latency.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_UWB_CIRDIAG
	bool "CIA/CIR diagnostics: first-path summary + windowed CIR tap dump"
	default n
	help
	  Latch the DW3000's CIA diagnostic bank on each reception (Ipatov/STS
	  first-path index, F1..F3, power, peak, STS quality, crystal offset) and
	  emit it as one "[ALAB] ev=uwb.diag" line from task context, plus an
	  independently-armed 64-tap Ipatov window around the first path
	  ("[ALAB] ev=uwb.cir").
	  The summary rides the `lab on` gate; `lab cir on` arms the taps and
	  `lab cir probe` runs a one-shot accumulator read diagnostic.
	  The window read is only valid while the receiver is idle, so it is taken
	  before the RX shim re-arms and only on one Final in four; even so it costs
	  walk-up latency while armed, so arm it only for a capture run. Set n to
	  strip it entirely.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_FLIGHT_RECORDER
	bool "Flight recorder: record a live walk-up for host replay"
	default n
	help
	  Record the DS-TWR responder's inputs (session config + every received
	  frame and its DW3000 register snapshot, in dispatch order) into a RAM ring,
	  dumped over the console with `fr dump` as hex "[FREC]" lines.
	  The capture converts to a .frc trace the host build replays
	  deterministically (tests/host/fr_replay.c).
	  OFF at boot; `fr on` before a walk-up, `fr off` after. Like the `lab` /
	  `uwbdiag` traces it reads extra registers while armed, so it costs walk-up
	  latency — arm it only for a capture run. Set n to strip it entirely.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_FLIGHT_RECORDER_BYTES
	int "Flight recorder RAM ring size (bytes)"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_FLIGHT_RECORDER
	default 16384
	help
	  Capacity of the static capture ring. One walk-up is a few dozen events at
	  ~40-170 bytes each; 16 KiB holds a full session with headroom. When the ring
	  fills, capture stops and the END record is flagged truncated.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_CRED_STEPUP
	bool "Enable the credential step-up (Access Document) phase"
	default n
	help
	  Reference-completeness build of the credential v1.0 §8.4 step-up phase: after
	  the standard EXCHANGE, the reader can request an ISO 18013-5 (mdoc) Access
	  Document from the User Device (ENVELOPE / GET RESPONSE APDUs carrying a
	  session-encrypted DeviceRequest/DeviceResponse), then parse and verify it
	  per §7.4 on a background worker.

	  This is OFF by default and, when on, is armed one-shot from the console
	  (`ultrawidelock-stepup arm`); it never runs per-unlock. The verification verdict is
	  logged and stored only -- it NEVER gates the unlock, which the provisioned
	  trust store alone decides. With this option unset the reader is byte-for-byte
	  identical to today and the fast-path auth segment is untouched.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_CRED_ACCESS_LISTENER
	bool "Expose the access verdict to a registered listener"
	default n
	help
	  Compile ultrawidelock_reader_set_access_listener() and the three notify points on
	  the credential trust gate, so an application can observe whether each credential
	  transaction was granted or denied. The listener gets the verdict and nothing
	  else, never a credential.

	  Selected automatically by the Home Assistant MQTT option; there is no reason
	  to set it by hand. Off by default because it is otherwise dead weight on the
	  transaction path: unset, the reader is byte-for-byte identical to today.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_PRESENCE
	bool "Presence assertions (non-door primitive)"
	default n
	help
	  Add a `presence` command to this board's console. `presence prove <nonce>`
	  ends old sessions and answers only after a new trusted authentication and
	  later UWB range; `presence pub` and `presence credential` emit the board
	  and pinned-human identifiers for enrolment. Proximity of a provisioned
	  iPhone, measured by UWB time of flight, becomes a proof a third party can
	  check -- no door involved.

	  The signing key is generated on first boot and persisted in NVS, so the
	  public point stays valid across reboots and reflashes. Alongside the normal
	  console, not instead of it, so the board a phone is provisioned against can
	  serve presence without being cloned.

	  Each check costs a phone wake plus roughly 3.5 s to a trusted range, so this
	  suits rare deliberate actions (signing a release tag), not anything
	  frequent or ambient. OFF by default.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_PRESENCE_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Fresh presence proof timeout (ms)"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_PRESENCE
	range 1000 30000
	default 8000
	help
	  End a proof without signing if a new authentication and trusted UWB range
	  have not completed in this window.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_PRESENCE_MAX_CM
	int "Maximum signed presence distance (cm)"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_PRESENCE
	range 0 3000
	default 40
	help
	  Firmware refuses to sign a fresh range beyond this distance. A verifier
	  may enforce an equal or lower threshold.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_RANGE_GATE_STRICT
	bool "Range gate: enforce STS quality for the lock path too (drop suspect blocks)"
	default n
	help
	  Enforce the layer-2 STS-quality check before a block reaches the range
	  store, so a poorly correlated block never feeds the unlock seam. Default
	  n = shadow mode: the verdict is logged (GATE line) and recorded with the
	  range, but the block still latches, so a mis-tuned threshold cannot lock
	  a human out of their own door.

	  This option governs the DOOR path only. Presence assertions
	  (ULTRAWIDELOCK_PRESENCE) always require a good STS and refuse to sign a range
	  without one, regardless of this setting, because a signed claim someone
	  else will act on has to fail closed where a lock must not.

	  Enable only after sizing FIRA_STS_QUALITY_MIN from bench captures. The
	  symbol previously existed only in the Zephyr Kconfig tree, so on ESP32
	  the gate could not be turned on at all.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_CRED_CLONE
	bool "Reader identity clone/export console commands (bench only)"
	default n
	help
	  Compile the `ultrawidelock-export` / `ultrawidelock-import` console commands, which
	  serialise the reader identity + trust store (INCLUDING the reader private
	  key) to a hex blob and load it onto a second board. This replicates a
	  provisioned reader so a phone's existing Wallet credential transacts with
	  the clone -- the bench setup for the presence-second-factor experiment
	  (export from the door reader, import on a dongle board).

	  OFF by default and never appropriate for a deployed reader: exposing the
	  private key on the console defeats the identity's whole purpose. Enable it
	  only on bench boards you control, for the duration of the experiment.

endmenu
